.TH CURS-ACCESS 1 "November 2026" "Curses Access 0.0.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
curs-access \- lettered menu launcher for the terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B curs-access
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B curs-access
shows a full-screen menu of up to 26 entries, lettered A to Z.
Each entry runs a program, through
.BR sh (1)
in its own working directory, or opens a sub-menu.
Sub-menus can be up to six levels deep.
When a program ends, the menu comes back.
.PP
Every screen follows the size of the terminal; the smallest supported
size is 64 columns by 20 lines.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no\-splash
Do not show the start-up logo.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-recover
Ask for the recovery code at start-up. If the code is right, the Exit and
Menu Maintenance passwords are removed.
.TP
.B \-\-reset\-security
Start over after confirming on the terminal: removes all passwords, makes
a new recovery code and deletes the encrypted usage log. Menus and
settings are kept.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-debug
Write a debug log to
.IR ~/.local/state/curs-access/debug.log .
The debug log never holds passwords, the recovery code or keys.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a short help text and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version and the encryption library in use, and exit.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B A\-Z
Run the entry, or open the sub-menu.
.TP
.B Up, Down, Home, End, PgUp, PgDn, Enter
Move the selection bar and choose.
.TP
.B Esc
Go back to the previous menu, cancel a dialog or leave a screen.
.TP
.B F1
Menu Maintenance (asks for its password if one is set).
.TP
.B F3
System Info.
.TP
.B F5
Show the logo.
.TP
.B F10
Exit (asks for the Exit password if one is set). In a sub-menu, F10
goes back to the main menu.
.TP
.B F4
At an Exit or Maintenance password prompt: use the recovery code.
.PP
.B Esc
followed by a digit (1\-9, 0) works as F1\-F10, for terminals that keep
the function keys for themselves.
.SH MENU MAINTENANCE
.TP
.B Create / Modify Main Menu
Edit entries in a table of description, directory and command.
Typing on a cell replaces it, and Enter edits it.
F1 Insert, F2 Delete, F3 Move, F5 Custom Title, F7 Tree,
F8 Options (pause after the program ends, entry password),
F9 Sub-Menu (makes the entry a sub-menu, or opens it), Esc back,
F10 back to the Maintenance menu.
.TP
.B Program Configuration
Command to run on exit, greeting, passwords, usage tracking, clock
display, 24-hour clock, menu border, screen blanking time, start-up logo,
color theme and each color.
Left and Right step through the values, and Enter changes a value.
.TP
.B Color Themes
Choose a built-in or saved theme, or save the current colors as a theme.
.TP
.B Usage Report
Decrypts and shows the usage log. F2 clears it.
.TP
.B Passwords & Recovery
Change the Exit and Maintenance passwords, or make a new recovery code.
.SH SIGNALS
At the menu, ^C, ^\, ^Z and ^S/^Q are read as ordinary keys and do
nothing, and SIGINT, SIGQUIT, SIGTSTP, SIGTERM and SIGTTOU are ignored.
SIGHUP restores the terminal and exits.
SIGKILL cannot be caught.
.PP
A program started from the menu runs in its own process group as the
terminal's foreground job, with default signal handling, so ^C and ^\
work in it as usual.
It cannot be suspended: if ^Z stops it, the menu resumes it at once.
.SH SECURITY
Passwords and the recovery code are stored only as one-way hashes:
Argon2id with libsodium, or scrypt with OpenSSL.
Usage log entries are sealed to an X25519 public key.
The matching secret key is stored twice, encrypted once with a key
derived from the Maintenance password and once with a key derived from
the recovery code.
The recovery code is shown once, when the program first starts, and
whenever a new code is made; it is never stored.
.PP
The passwords protect a shared terminal in the same way as the classic
DOS menu systems did; they are not a sandbox against a user who has a shell.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/curs-access/curs-access.conf
Program Configuration.
.TP
.I ~/.config/curs-access/menus.conf
The menus.
.TP
.I ~/.config/curs-access/security.conf
Password and recovery hashes, usage log keys.
.TP
.I ~/.config/curs-access/themes/*.theme
Saved color themes.
.TP
.I ~/.config/curs-access/logo.txt
Optional custom start-up logo; each line is drawn in the next rainbow color.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/curs-access/usage.log
Encrypted usage log.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/curs-access/debug.log
Debug log, written only with \fB\-\-debug\fR.
.PP
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME
and
.B $XDG_STATE_HOME
are respected when they are set.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Programs started from the menu get
.B CURS_ACCESS=1
in their environment.
